    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`. It builds and `go vet` is clean, but it hasn't been run against a database: no database credentials were available, and the repo has no tests (`go test ./...` passes only because there are none). I only changed `internal/candidate/`.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`operations.go`**: a new operation `reopenOp`. It is named `reopen.v5`, only a requester can run it, it only targets the caller's own purchases, and it moves status from `rejected` to `submitted`. It has no batch field, so the kernel returns `{\"id\":...,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`. It also writes a `purchase.reopened` event to the PurchaseEvent outbox, which the notify package already sends to the tenant's approvers.\n- **`approval.go`**: a new handler calls `executor.Run(ctx, u, reopenOp, key, []string{id}, reason)` and converts kernel rejections to protocol rejections with the existing `adapt`. The route is registered with `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", ...)`. Anything else falls through to the existing bulk-approve/bulk-reject handler, then to the original server, so those APIs behave as before.\n\nThe kernel and adapter provide the rest; I read their code but didn't test it:\n- **Validation:** `protocol.Item` returns 401 when unauthenticated and 403 for approvers. It also checks the request body, the reason length, the size limit and the Idempotency-Key.\n- **One transaction:** the kernel locks per tenant + actor + key. The status change, the audit row with the reason, the event and the ActionRequest row are all written together, so if the ActionRequest insert fails, nothing is saved and the response is 503.\n- **Ineligible target:** a purchase that isn't the caller's, isn't in this tenant, or isn't `rejected` gets 409 and nothing changes.\n- **Same key:** a replay gets `Idempotency-Replayed: true` and the stored response; the same key on a different purchase gets 409.\n\n**Decision for you:** if the same actor reuses the same key on the same purchase with a different reason, it is treated as a replay (200), not 409. The kernel's request hash covers only the operation name and the IDs, and the reconciliation job requires exactly that hash, so the reason can't be added to it. A requester also can't read the audit table where the reason is stored, so I couldn't check it from `internal/candidate/` either. The spec only requires 409 for a different purchase, so this meets it. If a changed reason must be rejected, the kernel or ledger would need to change, and those are protected."
    ],
